terested in is thinking about  trajectories across individuals  that might overlap diverge or  change to certain states that might  cause illnesses  but to be able to do this we need both  objective and  subjective measures now objective  measures are growing on a daily basis  with various kinds of wearables and  other  data sets that are coming online but  this is really about longitudinal data  about an individual  and we might we are hoping that we can  feed this  into some of our models and algorithms  that learn from the data  to provide better or more precise  indicators  to an individual or their caregivers and  this will require what  many people have said as deep  longitudinal phenotyping  in fact this figure on the right is from  about five years back  netapp a company that specializes in  storage  was already thinking about how the human  body would give  rise to a large amount of data  as time progresses and if you now think  of this as kind of longitudinal data  every single human is going to be  producing scales of data that you might  not have been thinking about previously  and this might be brain images and  signals  but it's also data from smartphones and  wearables  the genetic data especially if you now  consider transcriptomic and other types  of genetic transformations  are exploding at a rapid scale and size  clinical records are more accessible  although there's lots of  difficulties in getting to them right  now and then the word of  world of microbiomes is also changing  some of these scales of data as we speak  so how we preserve even our own  individual data is something we have to  think about  moving forward not just institutional  and structural  information that's stored in clinical  centers  and one of the projects i really  appreciate in this space is something  called  is a project called open humans which  allows  storing your own data into a personal  data store  and what this means is that you have  control over your data  you decide who to give it to you could  donate it for research you could do your  own research or you could explore  things by yourself and i think it's a  fantastic model for us to consider  as we move forward in thinking about  both privacy considerations but also  being able to aggregate  large amounts of data when we need to  solve problems  and this will still require the kind of  work that we were doing in the  neuroimaging data model but  the scale of all biomedical data we need  to be able to link  extract search over information even if  the raw underlying data may not be  accessible  we may need to compute over information  in privacy preserving ways as well  so it's something to keep in mind as we  think about the scale of data if we  could do this  maybe tomorrow well maybe not tomorrow  but maybe over a short period of time  we are able to ask questions for  specific disorders where there may not  be  large amounts of data in these public  archives we might be able to develop  algorithms and tools that actually  operate on real data and takes care  of bias across data sets and this will  require  access to data in a way and even open  humans where the model is fine  would still require evolving to the  scale and tune of data that's coming  online  and one of the projects in that space is  called the interplanetary file system  it's a way of distributing data across  nodes and networks  in a way where data is not just stored  in a central archive or a central  repository  but gets distributed to make certain  aspects of data access  and distribution much more efficient now  this may not be a solution for all  biomedical data tomorrow  but this is an architecture that one can  think about  in thinking about both private data sets  public data sets  and distributing data sets over the  planet  the final thing i'm going to end with  is the need for education  so on the left the missing semester of  your cs education is a website at mit  that teaches people about the things  that are not  often taught in courses  and these are critical in the world of  analytics and data systems  that exist today some of the things that  every student  should our researchers should know how  to be able to do is to make their com  computation portable to be able to go  from one system to another  to be able to transition from one  hardware architecture to another  there has to be a better way of doing  debugging  the iterative process that's often  involved in many of the analytic tools  especially deep learning and other  technologies requires us moving or  iterating on the problems  faster we have to be able to track  provenance if we have to show people  what a model was trained on how well it  can do  and metadata curation which is something  that's often ignored in most  analytics places needs to become  a more concerted effort and a systematic  effort across researchers and labs  we have to be able to compute  efficiently at the scales of data we are  seeing otherwise we will be waiting for  just computation to finish  bias and quality control are going to be  essential  with the rate at which data are emerging  a lot of data are being put out there  without uh  quality control and that's a good thing  in my opinion however as a community  we need to be able to label or annotate  the data sets  so that downstream users can decide  whether they want to use certain types  of data  for all of the software and analytic  tools testing and reproducibility and  validation are going to become critical  and for many domains where we don't have  ground truth available the community  kind of has to come together to  establish best practices  or principles by which we evaluate  not just from looking at the result and  whether it aligns with our hypothesis  but some more principle mechanisms  whether through  computational modeling or others to say  that what we are observing as a result  of the analytics  is actually what we want to see and  the scale of data is going to parallel  the scale of computing  which will go back to the kinds of tools  and technologies  that a student or a researcher might  need to learn today  to be able to deal with this explosion  of data that's happening  with that i'm going to thank you for  your attention and i'm  happy to take any questions sasha thank  you so much man you covered a ton of  ground that is really fantastic  um let me see couple of questions we  have one from  a one of our viewers here how do you  quantify  or mitigate uncertainty when deploying  machine learning techniques  in your data analytic quests are there  confidence intervals are those a big  deal should we be  paying attention to those uh  wonderful question so i can say about  one of the approaches we are taking  with bayesian neural networks which  as an inbuilt mechanism of that  architecture  provides us access to entropy or  uncertainty that we can leverage  so when it runs it kind of says  this is how certain i'm about what i'm  telling you  now many of these things as one might  know  could be confident about some things and  we haven't solved every problem with  uncertainty  but at least using these bayesian neural  networks  is one step of evaluating uncertainty  that we think can be useful  in the short term one thing that is  super  interesting about kind of the story you  just told is you know  kind of beginning with an effort i was  involved with  moons ago where we were delivering data  to people  on dvd and if you kind of think about  that time  uh it was about the same time that like  netflix  started to become popular and how did  you get your  movie of the week from you know from  netflix well you ordered it and they  delivered it on  on a media like a compact disc or or dvd  and so it wasn't unusual and we were  very sensitive to the fact that you know  filling up somebody's ethernet pipe with  you know streaming  neuro imaging data would very often  bring some networks to their knees  and so we actually specifically chose  that as a medium to act as kind of a  governor on the system right so that we  didn't have  one a lot of people complaining at us  that we you know  had basically having the investigators  being complained about that they'd  crash their own internets about it uh at  the same time  realizing that the the ability to stream  effectively that kind of data writ large  was going to be a real challenge  and we just the technology of the time  wasn't equipped for it  even netflix wasn't equipped now we take  that for granted right we stream stuff  to our phones  you know sitting on the on the bus or  something um  and so it that the technologies continue  to to move forward and that is certainly  something that we've seen  in neuroimaging with all the different  approaches and methodologies and  services  that that you mentioned and you know  things like this uh i hadn't heard of  the ipfs but you know you could imagine  being able to to do that  and i'm also was intrigued by the um  the net app talking about uh the  the netapp thing of 665 terabytes worth  of just  individual data that brought to mind the  quantified self  project which was uh kind of popular a  few years ago  but then linking into open humans if we  all quantified ourselves  that would be and you know made that  data distributed distributable through  ipfs and had all these quantitative  tools  it would really be quite an impressive  amount of stuff at our fingertips on  demand when we wanted it  it would be quite impressive absolutely  i i thought you might get a kick out of  that fmridc  uh slide over there jack  it was uh it was good you know it's  interesting it was it was  pioneering for its time um but uh i  just i marvel at what we started  um because there's just been so much  work done in this area which is just so  remarkable and you just summarized it  for us so  and one of the things i will say is uh  we are not  uh at the stage where everything can 